The automation bridge finally answers
===================================

`crates/quixi-app/src/main.rs` has had an automation bridge since upstream: a loopback HTTP server
where `GET /state` evals

    window.__quixi_automation__ ? JSON.stringify(window.__quixi_automation__.getState()) : {ok:false}

and `POST /action` evals `performAction(<payload>)`. Nothing in the page ever defined that object, so
both endpoints answered `{ok:false}` for everyone — and the only way to press a button was the system
pointer.

It exists now, and it is enough to drive the UI without touching a mouse:

  * `getState()` — page, title, which nav row is active, which sidebar groups are open, the visible
    panel heading, the composer text, and the labels of the first 40 buttons
  * `click` — by CSS selector, or by visible text (exact match first, then substring)
  * `type` — set a value and fire `input`, so the app's own listeners run
  * `open-group` / `navigate` — the two things a sidebar needs
  * `state` — the same read, through the action endpoint

Every action returns what it actually touched (`tag`, `text`, `class`, `connected`), so a driver can
tell a click that landed from one that silently missed.

Why this matters beyond convenience: pointer synthesis on Linux drives **the** cursor — there is one
per seat, and a second `uinput` device does not create a second one. Any agent that clicks by moving
the pointer is moving the human's mouse. Talking to the app through its own bridge is the only way to
operate this UI that structurally cannot do that.
